You are writing an independent pytest test file for a Python module you have
never seen the implementation of. Write tests strictly from the public
contract and the acceptance criteria below. Do not guess internal helpers.

## Public contract (this is all you get — no implementation source)

```python
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from typing import Callable

@dataclass
class Section:
    title: str
    level: int          # 0 for the document root and special rows; 1-6 for
                         # a real Markdown heading (H1-H6).
    own_text: str        # this section's own content, excluding children's
    children: list["Section"] = field(default_factory=list)


@dataclass
class Row:
    """One visible line, other than the root row: a section's title, its
    nesting level, its OWN token count (its own_text alone), and its TOTAL
    token count (own plus every descendant's, accumulated bottom-up)."""
    title: str
    level: int
    own: int
    total: int


def render(
    root: Section,
    count_fn: Callable[[str], int],
    total: int,
    frame: int = 0,
    fmt: str = "table",
    depth: int | None = None,
    sort: str = "document",
) -> str:
    """Render root's children as fmt, PLUS a root row for `root` itself.

    count_fn(text) -> int counts one section's own text (its `own` value).
    Every non-root row's `total` is the bottom-up accumulation: its own
    `own` plus every descendant's `total`, exactly as before — this is a
    subtree-relative weight, not guaranteed to be byte-exact against any
    external oracle for an interior node.

    `total` (the parameter): the caller supplies the REAL, authoritative
    total for the whole document (e.g. from a raw, un-subtracted count of
    the full source text) — the root row's `total` field is set to exactly
    this value, NOT derived by summing children (summing children would
    reintroduce a small, fixed, subtractive error — this parameter exists
    precisely so the root row is always exact regardless of that error).

    fmt: "table" (default) — the root row and every section row, WITH
    tree-drawing connectors (see below), followed by a blank line and a
    `boundary drift: ±N` line (see the drift formula below).
    "md" — an indented Markdown bullet list, root row first, each line
    showing both numbers, e.g. `f"{'  ' * level}- {title}: own={own}
    total={total}"` (root row: `"- {title}: own=0 total={total}"`, always
    `own=0` for the root since a Section's own `own_text` is never set to
    real root content by this parser).
    "csv" — header row `"level,title,own,total"`, then one data row for
    the root (level 0) followed by one row per section, in the same order
    "table"/"md" would visit them (root first, then depth-first,
    document order unless `sort="tokens"`. No drift line in csv — it stays
    strictly tabular.
    "json" — a single JSON OBJECT (not a bare array, unlike this module's
    previous version), parseable, shaped exactly:
    `{"total": <int>, "drift": <int>, "rows": [{"title":.., "level":..,
    "own":.., "total":..}, ...]}` — `rows` holds the non-root sections
    only (root's total is already the top-level `"total"` key); `drift`
    is computed with the same formula as the table/md footer line, see
    below.

    depth: if given, only rows with level <= depth are shown (root is
    always shown regardless of `depth`, since it's level 0 and depth is
    never negative) — their totals still include everything below the
    cutoff (rollup, never lost). This applies to json's `"rows"` and to
    csv's data rows too.
    sort: "document" (default, original order) or "tokens" (siblings
    reordered by their own accumulated `total`, descending — reorders
    each parent's children among themselves, never flattens/reorders
    across different parents).

    Tree-drawing connectors (fmt="table" only — "md"/"csv"/"json" keep
    their own existing conventions, described above, unchanged by this):
    a section that is the LAST child among its siblings is prefixed with
    `"└─ "`; every other child is prefixed with `"├─ "`. A line's ancestor
    chain contributes a vertical bar `"│  "` for each ancestor that still
    has later siblings of its own (i.e. that ancestor was NOT the last
    child at its own level), or three spaces `"   "` for an ancestor that
    was the last child at its level. The root row itself has no prefix.

    Boundary drift (applies to "table" — printed as a trailing line after
    a blank line —, "md" — same trailing line convention — and "json" —
    as the `"drift"` key; NOT "csv"):
    `drift = total - sum(row.own for row in <every non-root row>) - frame`
    — `frame` is the fourth parameter above, an explicit `int` the CALLER
    passes in (default `0`, meaning "no adjustment expected"). `render`
    itself has no way to know a "fixed per-chunk cost" on its own — it
    only ever calls the opaque `count_fn(text) -> int`, which could be
    anything — so this value always comes from outside. When the total
    and the sections agree exactly, once `frame` is accounted for (the
    healthy, expected case for this project's real tokenizer), `drift ==
    0`.
    """
```

## Why the root row, `frame`, and the drift formula work the way they do

This project measured, against a real oracle, that a whole document's true
token count is **not** exactly the sum of its section's own counts (each
counted with a fixed per-chunk cost subtracted) — it's that sum **plus
exactly one more instance of the fixed cost**. So a caller that wants the
root row to show the real, oracle-matching total has to pass it in directly
via the `total` parameter (computed by the caller from the raw, unsplit
document) rather than trust `render` to derive it by summing — summing
would come out exactly one `frame`'s worth short. The `drift` figure exists
to make this checkable at a glance, and it needs the caller to also pass
that same fixed cost in via the `frame` parameter (`render` cannot infer it
from `count_fn`, which is opaque): it should always land on `0` for a
healthy document once `frame` is supplied correctly, and a nonzero value is
a real signal that something is wrong (a section was skipped, the document
doesn't reconstruct cleanly, etc.), not an expected property of
the tool.

## Fixed test tree and count_fn (build/use this in every test that needs one)

```python
def make_tree():
    return Section(
        title="(document)",
        level=0,
        own_text="",
        children=[
            Section(title="Intro", level=1, own_text="intro text", children=[
                Section(title="Background", level=2, own_text="background text", children=[]),
            ]),
            Section(title="Conclusion", level=1, own_text="conclusion text", children=[]),
        ],
    )

FRAME = 2  # a plain test constant — NOT read by render() itself, only
           # ever used as the value you pass explicitly via render()'s
           # own `frame=` parameter, in the tests below that check drift.

def count_fn(text: str) -> int:
    # word count minus a fixed per-chunk cost, floor at 0 — deterministic
    # and easy to reason about by hand, and it lets FRAME be non-trivial
    # (not zero), same shape as the real Claude tokenizer's per-chunk cost.
    return max(len(text.split()) - FRAME, 0)
```

With this tree and this `count_fn`: `"intro text"` (2 words) → `own = max(2-2, 0) = 0`.
`"background text"` (2 words) → `own = 0`. `"conclusion text"` (2 words) →
`own = 0`. So `Intro.total = 0 + Background.total(0) = 0`,
`Conclusion.total = 0`. Sum of every non-root row's `own` = `0`.

For a nonzero, realistic `total`, use `total = 5` in the tests below (an
arbitrary but fixed "real" total the caller is asserting — you are not
computing it from the tree, you are supplying it, exactly as a real caller
would supply an externally-measured value).

Whenever a test checks the drift figure, call `render` with
`frame=FRAME` explicitly (`render` does not know `FRAME` on its own — you
are the caller supplying it, exactly matching the per-chunk cost your
`count_fn` used). With `total = 5, frame=FRAME (2)`:
`drift = 5 - 0 - 2 = 3` — **not** zero, on purpose, for tests that check the
drift formula is applied correctly (AC-15). For tests that need drift to
land on `0` (AC-16), use `total = FRAME` (i.e. `total = 2`, same `frame=2`):
`drift = 2 - 0 - 2 = 0`. Tests that don't check the drift line at all can
omit `frame=` (it defaults to `0`) — the drift figure is simply not
inspected in those tests.

## Test cases to write, one test (or more) per item

- **AC-05 / AC-12**: `render(tree, count_fn, total=5, fmt="table")` (also
  check `fmt` truly defaults to `"table"` — a call with no `fmt` produces
  identical output) — the output contains a line for the root (its title,
  `"(document)"`, and the total `"5"` somewhere on that line) AND separate
  lines for `"Intro"`, `"Background"`, `"Conclusion"` — assert each
  non-root section's line shows **two distinguishable numbers** (its `own`
  and its `total` both appear, and they are not the same rendered
  substring when they differ — for this tree they're all `0` for both, so
  additionally build a second tree where a parent has short own text and a
  child has long own text, and assert that parent's line shows a smaller
  `own` figure than its `total` figure, both present in the line).

- **AC-13**: `render(tree, count_fn, total=5, fmt="table")` — a root row is
  present, and its total is exactly `5` (the value passed in), NOT `0`
  (which is what summing the children would give with this `count_fn`) —
  this is the key assertion: root's shown total equals the injected
  `total` parameter, not the sum of the tree.

- **AC-14**: Build a tree where a parent section has short `own_text` (few
  words) and one child has long `own_text` (many more words) — using the
  `count_fn` above, pick word counts so the parent's own `own` is smaller
  than its `total` (own + child's total). Assert the parent's row shows
  `own < total` (two different numbers, in that order of magnitude).

- **AC-15**: `render(tree, count_fn, total=5, frame=FRAME, fmt="table")` —
  the output contains a line with the text `"boundary drift"` and the
  value `3` appears in that line (per the worked example above:
  `5 - 0 - 2 = 3`). The sign must be visible when negative too: repeat
  with `total=-3` (an edge case, `frame=FRAME` still) and assert the drift
  line shows a negative number (`-3 - 0 - 2 = -5`).

- **AC-16**: `render(tree, count_fn, total=2, frame=FRAME, fmt="table")` —
  the drift line shows exactly `0` (per the worked example: `2 - 0 - 2 =
  0`) — the output must not omit this line or claim a nonzero drift when
  it's actually zero.

- **AC-06**: `render(tree, count_fn, total=5, frame=FRAME, fmt="json")` —
  `json.loads` parses it into a `dict` (not a `list`, unlike the old
  contract), with a top-level `"total"` key equal to `5`, a `"drift"` key
  equal to `3` (same formula as AC-15), and a `"rows"` key that is a list
  containing entries
  for `"Intro"`, `"Background"`, `"Conclusion"` (root is NOT in `"rows"` —
  it's already represented by the top-level `"total"`), each with `"own"`
  and `"total"` fields.

- **csv regression**: `render(tree, count_fn, total=5, fmt="csv")` —
  parseable with `csv.reader`, header row is exactly
  `["level", "title", "own", "total"]`, the first data row is the root
  (`level="0"`, and its `total` column is `"5"`), followed by one row per
  section. No line containing `"drift"` appears anywhere in the csv output.

- **depth regression**: `render(tree, count_fn, total=5, depth=1)` — the
  output does NOT contain `"Background"` (level 2, cut off), but DOES
  contain the root row and `"Intro"` with its full accumulated `total`
  (rollup unaffected by depth, same as before this PBI).

- **sort regression**: build a tree where a third top-level sibling has a
  larger own-text word count than `"Intro"`'s subtree total, and assert
  with `sort="tokens"` that sibling appears before `"Intro"` in the
  output, and `"Intro"` before `"Conclusion"` (only reordering within the
  same parent, same as before this PBI).

- **AC-20**: build a tree with at least two levels of nesting and at least
  two children at the top level (e.g. reuse `make_tree()`), and render
  with `fmt="table"` (default): assert the output contains the connector
  characters `"├─"`, `"└─"`, and `"│"` somewhere (the exact layout is not
  asserted character-by-character — only that tree-drawing connectors are
  present, replacing plain space indentation). Also assert that `"md"` and
  `"csv"` output do NOT contain `"├─"` or `"└─"` — connectors are a
  `"table"`-only presentation choice.

